            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal> During the walkthrough, we found leakage on the following items:

-Small leakage from the front  old section window

-Receptacles

-Woodstove chimney

-High air leakage coming from the crawl space wall cavity through the floor grilles

-Rear and right doors leaks

We recommend the installation of weather strippings around doors and receptacles to increase the airtightness of this components. Concerning the floor grilles, it was hard to see from where the air infiltration is coming. This situation represents a high level of heat loss. The insulation of the crawl space walls might help to seal and fix the air leakage. Concerning the wood stove chimney leakage, there is no much to do. </airSeal>
                <mainWalls>We recommend to upgrade the original section insulation to meet the same as the new section.</mainWalls>
                <foundation>We recommend the insulation of the crawl space exterior walls. We suggest to do so from outside, this technique might help to reduce air leakage and will help to increase the airtightness. It can also fix the high level of air leakage felt during the infiltration test.</foundation>
                <HeatingSystem>At the moment of the assessment, the main heating system is a wood stove located in the basement. This type of system is not constant and requires a lot of effort to maintain the house warm. It might be a good thing to think about installing an electrical heating system like baseboards. The electrical baseboard might be able to ensure a more stable capacity of heating for the house, and also make it less demanding to heat the house. </HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>The depressurization test performed as shown a high risk of combustion gas spillage. Therefore, we recommend verifying your carbon monoxide detectors regularly.</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
